WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:02.630
SIERRA: Hello! So, If you listen to WIM you
know we talk a lot about China. Before we

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:03.630
started today, I wanted to let you guys know
about a new infoguide from CFR fellows Joshua

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:04.630
Kurlantzick and Yenjong Hwong. It’s called
“China’s Approach to Global Governance”.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.630
It takes a deep, visually rich dive into China’s
history, and its ambitious rise on the global

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.630
stage. Check it out on CFR.org. Thanks!

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.630
Politics 101: Ladies and gentlemen, nothing
is more important in our history.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.630
WHTV: How they wish to be judged by history.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.500
Washington Post: The stock market reached
yet another all time high today in history.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.710
Guardian: The longest stretch of job creation
in our history.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:16.715
CBS: The history of the United States.

00:00:16.715 --> 00:00:22.350
HAASS: History is not a cookbook. It doesn't
give you exact recipes to understand the present

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:27.170
or predictive future. But what it does is
give you context, it gives you comparisons,

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.029
it gives you perspective. History doesn't
repeat itself. But to use the old saw it does

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:33.029
rhyme.

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:36.750
GORDON-REED: Things that may seem perplexing
to you, have their roots in things that happened

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.059
in the past. It would be as if you were trying
to tell your story to someone, but you didn't

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:46.080
know where you were born. You didn't know
who your parents were. You didn't have any

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.620
memories of things that had happened when
you were a child. History consists of, of

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.829
things in the past that have had a bearing
on the present that you might wanna know.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:01:00.920
That help you explain why you're in the situation
you're in, why we are in a situation in a

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.020
given society.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.710
MACMILLAN: We need perspective in history.
Sometimes things that look very important

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.610
at the time turn out not to be that important.
And sometimes things that look very unimportant

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.280
turn out to be very important. History doesn't
reveal itself right away. And we need to think

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.170
about it. We need to look at the evidence,
we need to interpret it.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:25.360
Global News: Now, as the COVID 19 pandemic
continues to grow, so are the parallels being

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.960
drawn between it and another deadly virus
that struck the globe more than a century

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.960
ago.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.960
CBS: The pandemic of 1918.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.360
Al Jazeera: From the international monetary
fund, a stark warning.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.799
ITV News: It is the worst crisis since the
Great Depression.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:44.390
BBC: The Great Depression almost a century
ago.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.390
CNBC: The killing of George Floyd has sparked
nationwide protests, demanding an end to police

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.390
brutality.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:47.390
FOX: Nascar is banning the confederate battle
flag.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:52.420
ABC: We move on now to the showdowns across
America, protestors toppling statues and monuments

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.180
of confederate leaders and slave owners.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.280
CBS: [Protest Sounds]

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:06.390
SIERRA: There’s no doubt about it. We’re
in a situation right now. The things we’re

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.290
experiencing everyday just feel historic.It’s
not just a pandemic, not just a global economic

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.510
recession, and not just an anti-racism protest
movement that spread across the globe. But

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:20.739
dozens of world events that give a sense of
things changing -perhaps even falling apart.

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:25.700
There’s an old saying that those who don’t
understand history may be doomed to repeat

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.250
it. But, there’s also a lesser known saying
that those who don’t know history, lose

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:38.239
out on the opportunity to repeat the things
that worked. So we went out and found three

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:40.830
historians to try to help us understand what
history is for, and to explain what the past

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.030
can tell us about the current moment.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.950
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters. Today, does history matter? And what

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:50.510
type of history are we in right now?

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:57.379
SIERRA: It feels like right now history is
back. You know, it's been a long break for

00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:01.430
a lot of people, young people see themselves
going through things that, you know, they

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.489
only imagine their grandparents going through,
a quarantine, a, a potential economic depression.

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:10.349
So, what do you think history can tell us
about right now, about our current moment?

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:14.470
MACMILLAN: I think what history can tell us
about our current moment is that, you know,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.360
we are part of the human race, and we shouldn't
expect ... Although, I suppose we've got used

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.709
to expecting that everything's going to go
smoothly for us, and we don't need to look

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:23.739
backwards.

00:03:23.739 --> 00:03:29.150
SIERRA: This is Margaret MacMillan, a leading
historian and professor of history at Oxford

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:35.129
University. Her books have received the Hessell-Tiltman
Prize for History and the Samuel Johnson Prize,

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:36.420
among others.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.189
MACMILLAN: I think history reminds us that
combinations of circumstances, a sudden pandemic,

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:46.459
a sudden depression can throw all our assumptions
up in the air. And I think we're going through

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:51.810
this now, but I think what history will tell
us, and it can be reassuring, is that sooner

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:56.870
or later we do tend to get out of these things.
Sooner or later the Great Depression ended,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.000
and the world got back onto a more even keel.
So, we shouldn't despair, to remember that

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.950
humanity has been through many crises in its
long history, and has managed somehow to deal

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.730
with them and move on. But I think what history
can also tell us is the good ways, and bad

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:16.610
ways of dealing with crises, and when you
come out of a crisis, good ways and bad ways

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.970
of learning from it.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:24.979
HAASS: Generations are often defined by a
common experience that gives a cohort of people

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:30.970
an identity. So, we talk about maybe the civil
rights generation, or the Vietnam generation,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:36.440
or now it will probably be something about
the pandemic. Before that, might be 9/11,

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.470
might be Iraq. Maybe Afghanistan. Could be
for some the financial crisis.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.461
SIERRA: This is Richard Haass, president of
the Council on Foreign relations, and, it

00:04:45.461 --> 00:04:50.419
should be noted, my boss. But we didn’t
talk to him just because of that. Richard

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:55.660
has been on the front lines of history advising
President George H W Bush, running policy

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:01.080
planning for the State Department, and negotiating
peace in Northern Ireland as a special envoy.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.240
He also has a new book out that contains a
lot of history, called The World: A Brief

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.240
Introduction.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:14.389
HAASS: For my father, who was born in 1917,
it was the Depression. And he had me read

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:19.940
a book, when I was, came of age, called the
Invisible Scar. He said, "If you want to understand

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:25.300
me and my generation, you have to read this
book the Invisible Scar. Because this tells

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.800
you about how all of us who went through the
Depression, and in his case collected milk

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.650
bottles so they would have enough to eat.
You have, in order to understand me now, you

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.620
have to understand what I went through with
a lot of others." And I, uh, that, that had

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.720
an impression on me.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.960
SIERRA: So, I mean, the idea there is that
learning history helps you sort of escape

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:49.130
the bias of your own generation and go wider
and understand other ones.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.460
HAASS: Absolutely. Learning history gives
you a much broader, take I think. Because

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.630
all of us, by definition, have narrower experiences.
We're, limited by what we've read, what we've

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:02.300
studied, what we've personally experienced,
by our age. So you know, none of us can travel

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:08.729
back to ancient times, much less, 50, 60,
years ago. We weren't there. But what, what

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:12.380
happened then and there might have relevance
for now. What reading history gives you the

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:19.240
opportunity to do is to get into your time
machine. It's to go back and see for yourself.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.270
You can then take what you've seen through
the words or pictures of others. And then

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:29.230
that forms part of your mental construct,
almost your filter to change images through

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:34.231
which you look at. For example, the present,
or some slice of the present, that you're

00:06:34.231 --> 00:06:39.229
trying to better understand. It gives you
a different appreciation, a different perspective

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:44.639
on your own life. And also it helps you better
understand different generations.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:50.270
SIERRA: What periods of history have you been
thinking about as you consider the current

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.270
moment?

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:55.550
GORDON-REED: Well, this is a really interesting
moment because we have all of these things

00:06:55.550 --> 00:07:02.380
together.We have tremendous partisan conflict,
polarization in society. And of course, that

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:06.710
makes me think of the 1850s before we get
to the Civil War. We're hoping it's not that

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:07.740
bad.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:13.729
SIERRA: This is Annette Gordon-Reed, professor
of both law and history at Harvard, and the

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:19.110
recipient of the Pulitzer prize. She’s best
known for ground-breaking work on Thomas Jefferson

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:25.030
that revealed his parentage of children with
an enslaved woman, Sally Hemmings.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:33.139
GORDON-REED: You have the Spanish flu in the
beginning of the 1920s. And you have the Depression.

00:07:33.139 --> 00:07:39.849
And all these things are happening together.
This is strange. This is a very, very, singular

00:07:39.849 --> 00:07:43.570
moment. There are moments in history, there
are these sort of punctuation points that

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:49.509
come along and they bring about change, as
we seem to be, in that moment now. So, I think

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:55.389
I would have to combine eras. There's no one
time that makes me think of all of these things

00:07:55.389 --> 00:08:01.849
together. Someone was joking on Twitter a
couple of weeks ago that 2020 will be like

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:06.740
its own discipline. I mean, historians will
be able to say, "I'm a specialist in April

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:13.360
through June." Or whatever. Because, so much
has taken place in the last, you know, two

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.720
or three weeks. It’s different than it was
three months ago. We've forgotten about the

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.740
coronavirus. It hasn't forgotten us. It's
still there, but we've moved on to thinking

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:28.430
now about this whole question of police and
race and inequality in the United States.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:33.539
There are bits and pieces of different eras,
but all of this together. And the thing that

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:39.430
makes this different too from any other era
is the existence of social media. In which

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:46.470
people are connected in ways that even in
the past eras that seem like this are very,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:51.310
very different. I mean, the news cycle. We
used to talk it, about it being fast because

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.560
of cable news, the 24 hour. It's like-

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:54.560
SIERRA: Right.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.570
GORDON-REED: 24 seconds. I mean - 24-minute
cycles. A story can be born and grow up and

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:05.430
die in, in a matter of hours. We're in a brave
new world with all of this.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:10.079
SIERRA: So what do you think history can tell
us about this current moment?

00:09:10.079 --> 00:09:14.680
HAASS: Well, it's an important question. And
it's one I'm wrestling with. I recently wrote

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:19.180
a piece for our magazine, Foreign Affairs,
where I argued that what we were likely to

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.960
see was not a turning point in history, but
history's acceleration. That essentially all

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:29.670
the trends that were visible before this:
the worsening, say, of US-Chinese relations,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:33.660
the weakening of the European, project. And
we can, we can go around the world. A lot

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.959
of weak states, potentially failed states.
I thought all of those would be present now

00:09:37.959 --> 00:09:43.899
or in the future. But in spades. Even more.
That this would intensify or accelerate history.

00:09:43.899 --> 00:09:49.040
And we'll see whether I'm right. What also
worries me, and I think I ended the piece

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.060
with this, is the historical parallels. Where
would this leave the world? And there were

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:56.329
some people out there in the conversation
saying, "Oh, this is terrible. But there's

00:09:56.329 --> 00:10:02.160
going to be a silver lining. We're all going
to learn the lesson of, uh, the futility of,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.820
uh, ignoring global challenges like pandemics
or climate change or what have you and we're

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:12.839
going to strengthen all of our global institutions."
And I shrug my shoulders and go, "Maybe."

00:10:12.839 --> 00:10:17.420
Now that did happen once in modern history.
Which was right after World War II. You had

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:23.850
an extraordinary period of institution building,
alliances through the World Bank, the IMF,

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:31.190
the UN. And it set the stage for 70, 75 years
of really unprecedented peace, prosperity,

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.480
uh, democratic development.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:39.709
SIERRA: The post WWII era was exceptional,
and among historians it seems to be the go-to

00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:44.839
choice for an optimistic precedent for our
current, chaotic times. Then, too, it felt

00:10:44.839 --> 00:10:49.790
like the world had fallen apart, that the
old way of doing things was gone. Look, of

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.579
course not everything was perfect. But the
world stepped up to the plate. Led by the

00:10:54.579 --> 00:10:59.460
United States, a series of structural changes
brought about increasing prosperity and relative

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:00.970
global peace.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.779
MACMILLAN: We know why the second World War
broke out. There's very little debate about

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:10.950
its origins, and it broke out because of extremism.
It broke out because you had highly nationalistic

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:15.980
governments motivated by a variety of ideologies
who want to extend their power, and, and conquer

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:20.350
others. And it was a catastrophe for the world.
But out of it came something I think much

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.690
better. I mean, I think those who, who led
the allies to victory in the second World

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:28.649
War recognized that they needed to build a
different sort of international order, and,

00:11:28.649 --> 00:11:33.660
and it was hoped to avoid a third World War.A
lot of people felt that if there was a third

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:37.040
World War that there would be very little
left of human civilization. So, they were

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:42.120
acting I think under a tremendous sense of
urgency. And what they did is try and avoid

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:47.399
the mistakes of the 1930s. And so, Roosevelt
took the lead in setting up the United Nations.

00:11:47.399 --> 00:11:52.639
So, I think we did learn, and our leaders
did learn from the second World War. And in

00:11:52.639 --> 00:11:58.580
Europe you saw national leaders recognizing
that Europe could not go through another World

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:03.100
War if it was to survive in any shape or form.
And so, you had European leaders from different

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:07.670
countries extraordinarily from France and
Germany, for example, coming together after

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:12.420
the second World War and saying we need to
set up some sort of supranational organization

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:16.940
which will overcome the poisonous nationalisms,
which have so nearly destroyed Europe and,

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:23.070
and encouraged us to work together. And that
of course was the origins of the European

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:29.160
Union. And so, I think lessons were learnt,
and real change was made after the second

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:33.910
World War. And real change was made domestically
as well. I mean, a number of governments recognized

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:37.389
that there were deep inequalities in their
societies, and they had to do something about

00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:43.180
it. And so, you've got a raft of social welfare
measures of free education, of support for

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:47.650
change in society, which really made a difference.
And you could see it playing out through the

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.589
1950s and 1960s.

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:55.240
SIERRA: It can be all too easy to forget history,
and to think things have always been the way

00:12:55.240 --> 00:13:00.510
they are now. In the U.S. for example, we’ve
become so used to a gridlocked congress and

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:05.590
partisan conflict, that the idea of two nations
- like Germany and France - working together

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:10.139
just a few years after facing off in a bloody
war - seems like a fairy tale. But it can

00:13:10.139 --> 00:13:11.899
happen. And it did.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:17.279
MACMILLAN: The trouble is that as great catastrophes
fade into the past, we tend to forget why

00:13:17.279 --> 00:13:21.010
we wanted the changes we wanted. And we tend
to forget why we thought it was so important

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:25.570
to have international organizations, and to
have domestic reform, and to try and build

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:29.910
more equal and just societies. And that I
think is a bit what's happened to us in the,

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:34.591
in the last decades. We've forgotten why we
needed some of those institutions, and perhaps

00:13:34.591 --> 00:13:38.290
we're going to remember again why we do need
them.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:44.670
HAASS: It was probably the most, by far, most
creative period of American foreign policy

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:48.640
in the modern era. So some were saying, "Well,
that's the parallel." And I hear that and

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:53.529
I go, "From your mouth to God's ear. But unlikely."
And if you look at all the, the differences

00:13:53.529 --> 00:13:58.589
between then and now-I'm struck more by the
differences than the similarities. The only

00:13:58.589 --> 00:14:02.160
similarities we, we've come out of something
terrible. That was World War II. Now it was,

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.259
will be the pandemic. To me, the parallels
are much more after World War I.

00:14:06.259 --> 00:14:12.399
SIERRA: The era after World War I, and particularly
the Great Depression of the 1930s, shows that

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:16.730
global crises don’t automatically lead to
cooperation and improvements.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:20.690
HAASS: Again you're coming out of something
terrible. But you, how do you, United States,

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:25.889
that was distracted, essentially was, uh,
becoming more, uh, isolationist. You had a

00:14:25.889 --> 00:14:30.240
lot of dark forces in the world. Uh, opposition
to immigration. A lot of opposition to, to

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:37.570
free trade. Which led to a heavy rise in protectionism.
And again, I'm not saying now that the situation

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:42.870
will inexorably lead from where it was after
World War I for two decades that led us to

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.180
World War II. So I'm not saying-

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.180
SIERRA: Oh good.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:50.329
HAASS: Yes. Uh, as dark as I am, I'm not that
dark. But history provides a warning here.

00:14:50.329 --> 00:14:54.320
MACMILLAN: There was a lot of talk that perhaps
democracy had failed because the democracies

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:59.959
didn't seem to be able to cope with mass unemployment,
and, and the terrific economic downturn, and

00:14:59.959 --> 00:15:04.769
nor did capitalism seem to be working. And
so, I think there was a real sense that the

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:09.459
pillars of what we felt were Western liberal
societies were crumbling, and nobody knew

00:15:09.459 --> 00:15:13.880
what was going to take their place, which
is why of course things like Bolshevism, the

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:18.880
type of, of communism that, that grew up in,
in Russia, that then the Soviet Union, and

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.959
the various varieties of fascism, from Italian
fascism to, to Nazism in Germany seemed so

00:15:23.959 --> 00:15:29.519
attractive because they seemed to be solving
the problems of mass unemployment, deep divisions

00:15:29.519 --> 00:15:33.991
in society. And so, it was a time in which
there seemed to be no clear solution, but

00:15:33.991 --> 00:15:39.639
a tremendous loss of faith in existing institutions,
which drove in many countries, people towards

00:15:39.639 --> 00:15:44.060
the extremes to the right and the left with,
with really dangerous consequences.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:49.160
SIERRA: There are uncomfortable parallels
here with our own time. The United States

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:54.380
has retreated from several international agreements
and institutions, including, potentially,

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:59.290
the World Health Organization. The UK has
left the European Union. It’s hard to look

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:04.459
at the news and say that the world is doing
a good job of solving problems together.

00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:09.890
So, the idea there is that having a strong
understanding of that period might protect

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:11.769
us now from similar temptations.

00:16:11.769 --> 00:16:15.830
MACMILLAN: Well, I think what that period
shows is that you shouldn't go for simple

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:19.550
solutions. I mean, leaders will come along
who say, "Look, it's all very clear. I can

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:24.190
get you all back to work. It's all gonna be
fine. Don't worry. I have a solution." And

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:29.230
that I think we now know is very dangerous
indeed. I don't trust the leaders who offer

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:32.740
very simple solutions to very complicated
problems.

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:35.509
SIERRA: Right. I mean, people love easy answers.

00:16:35.509 --> 00:16:38.790
MACMILLAN: We love easy answers and I think
we're seeing it today. You know, we're seeing

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:43.610
a proliferation of conspiracy theories, and
it's a natural human instinct. You know, when

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:48.209
something goes badly wrong, we want someone
to blame, and we like to think there's a cause,

00:16:48.209 --> 00:16:51.139
because at least if you can find who's to
blame, and there's a cause you have some hope

00:16:51.139 --> 00:16:55.780
of, of dealing with it. But it's a misguided
attempt I think because you know, in something

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:59.760
like this, the, the pandemic or in something
like the Great Depression, there is no single

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:05.030
cause, and there certainly is no single group
to blame. And I think chasing after conspiracy

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:09.410
theories doesn't help us think constructively
about what we need to do. We shouldn't be

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:13.260
looking for blame at this stage, we should
be looking for what we can do, and how we

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:15.300
can get out of this present situation.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:19.780
SIERRA: Do you think that democracy could
be at risk once again?

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:23.740
MACMILLAN: It depends, I think, very much
on the society, but yes, I think democracy

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:28.750
is clearly at risk in certain countries. In
Hungary, Viktor Orban, the president, has

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:33.980
used the opportunity to give himself power
to rule by decree. In Brazil, I think Bolsonaro

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:40.190
is, is undermining democracies, is, is not
using a democratic means, not, not following

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:46.070
the rules at all. In India, I think. In Turkey
I think we see pre- the press being attacked.

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:51.420
We see civil rights being, being abused. And
so yes, I think people do use the opportunity,

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:56.090
but in other countries I think we see democracy
is actually functioning very well. We see

00:17:56.090 --> 00:18:02.150
that in Germany, Angela Merkel has absolutely
managed to bring along the German people,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.860
and I think German democracy will come stronger
out of it because it has actually been very

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:10.960
successful in dealing with the epidemic. But
I think around the world there, there is going

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:15.280
to be a real sort of testing of different
kinds of government. And we will have to see

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:20.200
which come out better. But I'm confident that
democracy will show itself in the long run

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:25.720
to be capable of dealing with such challenges
more effectively on the whole than authoritarian

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:26.720
governments.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:32.590
SIERRA: OK, so, we have two very different
examples of what can happen when the world

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:37.650
feels like it has come apart. In the case
of the period after World War II, people came

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:42.590
together. Huge structural changes were put
into place, and things changed for the better.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:48.040
In the case of the 1930s and the Great Depression,
the opposite unfolded. Nations turned inward,

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:54.120
and away from democracy, minorities were scapegoated,
and catastrophe ensued. As historians grapple

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:59.250
to understand what direction we’re headed
in now, one surprising development might provide

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:05.190
a clue: the rapid change in public opinion
about racial justice in the United States.

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:09.980
MACMILLAN: I wouldn't have expected this a
couple of years ago, but I think what's happened

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:16.030
is possibly the pandemic, possibly the failures
of, of the Trump administration to deal with

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:21.820
it, and other issues and worries about immigration,
have somehow crystallized and come together

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:26.340
with the killing of George Floyd to make people
say that there is something we really need

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:30.150
to change about our own society. And it seems
to me that there is a willingness on the part

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.400
of, of Americans. I mean, there were always
people who want change, but it seems to me

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.870
now there's a much more widespread feeling
that, you know, we really do need to make

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:40.610
change. And I'm certainly noticing it here
in the United Kingdom as well.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:45.260
SIERRA: A lot of journalists and historians
of the civil rights movement have been noting

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:50.450
that, you know, we've seen scenarios similar
to this before, several times, since the beginning

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:54.630
of the 20th century. And there's debate about
whether or not this instance of unrest is

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:58.309
any different, you know? Whether it will succeed
where others failed. I mean, what do you say

00:19:58.309 --> 00:20:00.670
to the idea that history is repeating itself
here?

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:05.350
MACMILLAN: Well, I would say history never
repeats itself entirely because things have

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:10.400
changed, the context has changed. And I think
with anything like civil rights, what tends

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:15.030
to happen is you make progress as a society
and then, and then there is backsliding or,

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:20.200
or moving away from that progress. So it's,
it's not an unbroken march towards a better

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.970
society. I don't think history ever, ever
works like that. But it may be too soon to

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:27.980
tell, historians never like to try and judge
their own times when things are happening

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:34.820
right away, but my sense is that there is
more widespread support for significant change

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:39.570
in American society and in some other societies
than there was in the 1960s. I mean, I think

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:44.050
we have to remember in the 1960s, the United
States was very divided about how much change

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:49.640
it wanted. And Richard Nixon got into office
in the election of, of, of 1968, partly because

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:53.690
he talked about appealing to a silent majority
who didn't like all the sorts of changes that

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:58.550
were going on. It seems to me there is a more
widespread movement which goes across, not

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:02.270
just the cities. I mean, it's, you know, there
are demonstrations in small towns. Um, there

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:07.130
are demonstrations in places where you wouldn't
expect to see them in support of George Floyd

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:11.220
and, and demonstrations critical of the police
in places you wouldn't expect to see. So it

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:15.690
seems to me that possibly now we're seeing
something that really is a democratic and,

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:17.640
and movement supported at the grass roots.

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:21.560
GORDON-REED: Oh yes. Uh, this is, uh, a punctuation
point that I was talking about. I referred

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:27.400
to before, a moment when people kinda stop
and say, "Wait a minute, we have to do things

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:33.990
differently." And that has been sort of a
lightning fast change from the notion that

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:38.549
you have to support the police unquestioningly.
It's not like just supporting the, the police,

00:21:38.549 --> 00:21:45.810
but without question. To now saying, "Yeah,
we have to, to a degree, but we also have

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:51.179
to start thinking about the right balance
between police power and citizen power." And

00:21:51.179 --> 00:21:56.800
yeah, I mean, the majorities now are, are
clear that Americans are ready for some different

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:02.450
way of, of policing and basically determining
how that, that relationship, police to citizen

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:06.150
relationship will, will go forward. And that's,
that's new.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:11.470
HAASS: Again, history doesn't necessarily
repeat itself. There is the potential for

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:17.470
change. And in areas where reform is called
for you hope not only is there the potential

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:19.539
for change, but you hope that potential is
realized.

00:22:19.539 --> 00:22:23.630
SIERRA: So, it's safe for me to just say,
"We're living in a historic moment." You think

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:24.630
I'm good on that?

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:28.179
HAASS: I think you're really good on that.
I actually feel we are living in history.

00:22:28.179 --> 00:22:33.580
Uh, it's a phrase I like to use. I feel it
domestically for the reasons we're talking

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:40.539
about, from the pandemic to the protests in
the wake of the killing of George Floyd, the

00:22:40.539 --> 00:22:44.740
scale of the unemployment. But I actually
feel we're also living in history internationally.

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:49.789
This is the Council on Foreign Relations.
And I look at China's rise. I looked at the

00:22:49.789 --> 00:22:54.900
events in Europe. I look at, uh, the United
States pulling back from any number of international

00:22:54.900 --> 00:23:00.520
arrangements. I've got to tell you it doesn't
make me feel good. Most of history to me is

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:07.040
a fairly depressing, uh, undertaking. And
to me, we're seeing the United States, once

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:13.210
again, move in the direction of isolationism.
People are focusing on things internally,

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:18.570
rather than externally. It may be understandable,
but it's still consequential. Uh, I see the

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.770
difficulty, not just the United States in
the world, are having with coping with the

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:26.770
pandemic. I see virtually no signs that the
world was contending with climate change.

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:30.770
Terrorists haven't gone away. North Korean,
nuclear weapons and missiles haven't gone

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:36.590
away. Iran hasn't changed it's stripes. We've
got all the refugees and internally displaced.

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:41.409
I could go on and on and it makes for quite
a list and it's, it's not a happy list. So

00:23:41.409 --> 00:23:44.429
yeah, I do feel we're living history, but
more than anything else, it's because of the

00:23:44.429 --> 00:23:49.889
United States, which for 70, 75 years has
played this outsized role in the world, where

00:23:49.889 --> 00:23:54.781
we've essentially abdicated. And it's a bad
combination that you've had this emergence

00:23:54.781 --> 00:23:59.289
of, uh, a reemergence of familiar foreign
policy issues. You've got the emergence of

00:23:59.289 --> 00:24:04.391
new global issues, and you've got a United
States that is reluctant to act. And this,

00:24:04.391 --> 00:24:10.200
this is a toxic combination. And the tragedy
is here we are, after the pandemic began in

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:16.280
Wuhan. You would think the big lesson would
be, that nothing stays local for long. And

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:21.350
this outbreak of disease in this city that
very few had ever heard of would wake us up

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:26.029
to the fact that what happens in the world
matters. To coin a phrase that has something

00:24:26.029 --> 00:24:30.179
to do with this, uh, this podcast. And I worry
that people are not drawing the linkages.

00:24:30.179 --> 00:24:33.809
SIERRA: Not considering history during a historic
moment is not only bad for governments or

00:24:33.809 --> 00:24:35.790
nations or societies, but for individuals
too. Examining past decisions or paths makes

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:40.390
you better equipped to move forward with fresh
ideas, or repeat good ones that worked the

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:46.149
first time around. If nothing else, it makes
you better equipped for an argument, or to

00:24:46.149 --> 00:24:49.110
offer up a suggestion at a key time.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:53.760
HAASS: I'll give you a specific story where
I actually was able to quote, unquote, use

00:24:53.760 --> 00:25:01.059
history or apply it. It was, uh, 1991. In
the summer of 1990, Saddam Hussein, Iraq invaded

00:25:01.059 --> 00:25:05.520
Kuwait. I was the Chief Middle East Advisor
on the National Security Staff at the White

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:11.650
House. So I was heavily involved in it. Uh,
by the middle of January 1991 we had begun

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:16.799
the counter attack. Essentially Desert Shield
gave way to Desert Storm. Uh, and then towards

00:25:16.799 --> 00:25:21.460
the end of February we had transitioned from
just an air war to a ground war and things

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:26.559
were going very fast and going very well.
So by late February the question came up when

00:25:26.559 --> 00:25:31.140
do we stop? What do we have to accomplish
before we stop? We had our war aims, which

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:35.870
was to liberate Kuwait. But at that point
things were going so well that there were

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:40.679
certain voices saying, "Maybe we should go
on. Go on to Baghdad. Change the government

00:25:40.679 --> 00:25:46.080
and so forth." And I wrote a memo at the time.
Uh, about what happened in the Korean War.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:50.539
In the 1950, in the Korean War, uh, the United
States, uh, through the United Nations had

00:25:50.539 --> 00:25:55.500
gotten involved after the North Korean invasion
of the south. We liberated the south. And

00:25:55.500 --> 00:25:59.120
then the question came up, under President
Truman and General MacArthur, whether to stop

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:05.049
there or not. And then the flush of-Uh, tactical
success, we went north of the 30th Parallel.

00:26:05.049 --> 00:26:11.559
Went up close to the Chinese border to the
Yalu River. And ignored warnings of what would

00:26:11.559 --> 00:26:17.521
happen. Hundreds of thousands of Chinese quote,
unquote volunteers, IE soldiers came in. Two

00:26:17.521 --> 00:26:21.480
years later there was a stalemate back at
the 38th Parallel. We had gained nothing.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:26.330
But we lost another 20,000 lives. And what
I wrote was a memo to President Bush, the

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:32.740
father, about this. About the risk of expanding
war aims in the middle of a war. And I essentially

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:38.330
argued that we should think not just twice
but three times before expanding war aims.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:42.539
We shouldn't go on to Baghdad. We didn't have
the political backing for it. And here was

00:26:42.539 --> 00:26:49.430
a piece of history that said be very, very,
very careful before you do that. Now I can't

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:53.570
sit here and tell you that's what turned the
president around. But, uh, at least for me,

00:26:53.570 --> 00:27:00.140
I felt really comfortable in an ability to
bring history to bear on what proved to be

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.450
not just an important decision, but I think
history, subsequent history bears out was

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.850
the correct decision.

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:13.080
SIERRA: This is, of course, just one example
- the U.S. has not always acted with such

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:18.220
restraint. Careful consideration of history
can help slow things down, and avoid catastrophe.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:19.740
But that doesn’t mean that it has always
been used for good.

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:23.960
SIERRA: So, do you think that lessons from
history can actually be misused?

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:29.011
MACMILLAN: Oh, I think yes. You have to be
careful with history. The pro- the problem

00:27:29.011 --> 00:27:34.470
with history and its lessons is you can basically
find any lesson you want in history. And there's

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:39.770
an awful lot of history there, and there are
an awful lot of lessons. And I think unscrupulous

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:44.720
leaders, or deluded leaders have used history
to try and justify what they're doing. And

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:49.350
it sounds good. You know, if they say to their
people, "Our history tells us that, you know,

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:53.730
such and such has always been our enemy, and
therefore we have to behave in certain ways."

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:58.970
History gives a fake veneer of authority often
to very bad policies. I think the other thing

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:04.159
where history is bad, of course, is when people
remember, and remember historical grievances.

00:28:04.159 --> 00:28:10.149
You can get memories that go back, which keep
distrust alive between peoples, and which

00:28:10.149 --> 00:28:11.350
can be very unproductive.

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:17.230
GORDON-REED: A bad way of thinking about history
is that, you know, you hear the word revision-

00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:21.850
all the time, "Well, that's "... revisionist."
And it's said in a pejorative way, but all

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:27.490
history is revising things. You look at things
and you f- get new information. That's what

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:31.830
history is about. You're always gonna find
things that change the way you think about

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:37.720
stuff. So... I, I would say, you know, this
disinclination to accept new stories grows

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:42.640
out of a mistaken impression that the history
that people wrote 60 years ago was right.

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:49.590
And now you're just coming along and upsetting
that and changing the story. No, you are broadening

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:54.021
the story. You're revising it. Certainly the
rewriting of the story of reconstruction,

00:28:54.021 --> 00:28:55.021
in which people painted it as, uh, an era
where black people were ruling over white

00:28:55.021 --> 00:28:56.021
people, and black people who couldn't read
or write, or passing ridiculous laws and doing

00:28:56.021 --> 00:28:57.021
all kinds of things. That's not true. You
can, if you can tell a history, if you could

00:28:57.021 --> 00:28:58.021
make up a story and tell bad history, you
can influence people. So yeah, uh, using the

00:28:58.021 --> 00:28:59.021
idea of history can certainly be misused.
If people are trying to do history that is

00:28:59.021 --> 00:29:00.021
contextually sound and evidence-based, we're
all gonna make mistakes. That's one thing.

00:29:00.021 --> 00:29:01.021
But if to use the mantle of history as, as,
uh, as propaganda, is problematic.

00:29:01.021 --> 00:29:02.021
SIERRA: So I know recently, uh, you've written
or over the, over the course of time, you've

00:29:02.021 --> 00:29:03.021
written about, um, the recent trend of crowds
tearing down statues of Confederate leaders.

00:29:03.021 --> 00:29:04.021
So I'd love to know, uh, you know, what do
you think statues and monuments say about

00:29:04.021 --> 00:29:06.320
a culture's relationship to history?

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:11.490
GORDON-REED: Well, it, that's a very, very
good question. Because, people have been relating

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:16.179
it to history. You know, when someone says,
"You're taking down a statue, you're destroying

00:29:16.179 --> 00:29:21.200
history." Well, not really. I mean, that's
not how most people learn history. We don't

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:27.470
learn history through statues. We learn history
typically in school, through our own reading

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:32.350
and so forth. You can know about all of these
people, all the statues could disappear and

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:36.799
we would still know (laughs) about, uh, the
important figures of history. But statues

00:29:36.799 --> 00:29:42.890
do sort of send a message about a particular
community's values. And what we've been arguing

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:49.789
about is, you know, are there some people
who are unfit, or too problematic, or too

00:29:49.789 --> 00:29:58.490
divisive to be the bearer of a message? The
Confederacy's announced purpose was to maintain

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:04.890
white supremacy and African slavery. That
African people, people of African descent,

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:10.491
were meant to be enslaved. And for my values,
or my belief, I, I don't think that that is,

00:30:10.491 --> 00:30:16.710
those are ideas that we want to promote in
the public sphere. They lost the war. And

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:22.330
there was a hope that we could move beyond
that with the 13th, 14th, and 15th amendments.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:27.490
We know it's been a struggle, but the ideal
has been to move beyond the Confederate's

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:30.919
conception of the way the world works.

00:30:30.919 --> 00:30:35.740
SIERRA: So, you've taught history for a long
time. You know, what are some flaws that you've

00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:38.640
seen in the way we are taught history and
why it matters?

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:46.320
GORDON-REED: Well, I think one of the things
that we do is to think of it in terms of facts

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:49.530
and dates and things that are kind of set
in stone. And I don't think that people understand

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:50.530
enough or think enough about the fact that
history or historiography, that is writing

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:51.530
about history, is a continuum, continual process
of revision. As you find new facts, as you

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:55.159
ask different questions. So the idea of the
past is something that is fixed and static

00:30:55.159 --> 00:30:56.431
is problematic. And I think that may be one
reason that a lot of people or many people

00:30:56.431 --> 00:30:57.431
don't begin to appreciate history until they
are older. They think of it as, as I started

00:30:57.431 --> 00:30:58.431
out saying, facts and dates and hard, concrete
things that you have to remember and are set

00:30:58.431 --> 00:30:59.799
in stone, when it's very fluid. And lots of
things are going on at the same time, it's

00:30:59.799 --> 00:31:04.630
like a river. Different currents going different
ways and teasing all of that stuff out, to

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:06.630
me at least, is what makes history fun and
exciting.

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:07.630
SIERRA: Hm. And of course, it changes depending
who's telling that history, right?

00:31:07.630 --> 00:31:08.630
GORDON-REED: Absolutely, absolutely. Every,
you know, different people ask different questions.

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:09.630
And certainly, different generations ask different
questions of material. Based upon the things

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:10.630
that are going on around them. Historians,
the thi- if the, the Vietnam war, World War

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:11.630
II. All of these things, you see it in terms
of, of the current, you know, the current

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:12.630
crisis. Whether you even a current crisis,
whether you're sort of acknowledging that

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:13.630
or not, whether you know it or not-it shapes
your world.

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:14.630
SIERRA: So you've probably graded a lot of
papers. What grade would you give, you know,

00:31:14.630 --> 00:31:15.640
human society on their, uh, interpretation
and learning from history?

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:16.700
GORDON-REED: History, I don't know. Well-
I don- I'm, I'm a pretty lenient grader, uh,

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:17.700
generally. A B, B minus something like that.
I, I wouldn't fail us.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:18.700
SIERRA: That's not bad.

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:19.700
GORDON-REED: No, that's not bad. No, it's
not bad. I, I think, I think it depends on

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:20.700
what kind of history. I mean, in, in America,
we focus on, well, there, there's so many

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:22.610
... Well, there's sort of different, different
parts of history. I, I would give ourselves

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:25.140
a B minus. I, I would be generous on that
one.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.580
SIERRA: Well, that's good. I like that one.
I'll take that grade.

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.840
GORDON-REED: But, you know, it's really frightening
to me to think of the sort of retrenchment.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:36.669
People thinking you don't need history classes
and, you know, it's all STEM, STEM, STEM.

00:31:36.669 --> 00:31:43.270
Which I think is very, very important, but
for citizenship, for good citizenship, you

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:47.992
need to have an understanding of, of history.
And so, that's the only thing that I, that

00:31:47.992 --> 00:31:54.179
concerns me now is the, the sort of marginalizing
this as a soft subject, when it is a critical

00:31:54.179 --> 00:31:55.539
subject.

00:31:55.539 --> 00:32:00.010
MACMILLAN: History is not just something that
lies there like a great dead matter in the

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:04.400
past. It's something that weaves into our
lives. And, and has created the world in which

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:08.380
we live. And therefore, I think if we want
to know more about our own world, we need

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:09.460
to know the history.

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:13.389
MACMILLAN: I think it can tell us that nothing
bad lasts forever. Although, it may also tell

00:32:13.389 --> 00:32:17.789
us that nothing good lasts forever. And I
think it can tell us that, you know, human

00:32:17.789 --> 00:32:22.299
beings are enormously resilient. I mean, you
look at what we've come through, and you think

00:32:22.299 --> 00:32:26.460
of the ancestors of many of the people in
the United States, what they have suffered,

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:30.460
wherever they came from, whether they were
brought as slaves from Africa, whether they

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:35.130
came as impoverished immigrants from Europe.
And you think of the challenges of living

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:39.639
in this country, certainly, before the modern
age. And people survived and managed. And

00:32:39.639 --> 00:32:43.299
I think that sort of comfort is there in history
that we are resilient.

00:32:43.299 --> 00:32:49.720
HAASS: That's what's so good about history.
There's, there's parallels which don't necessarily

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:54.850
give you a bottom line in how to understand
something or what to recommend. But they can

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:57.480
sure give you, you know, they can sure give
you guidance. And, again, that's where it's

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:01.970
a tool. It's a, I think there's a, a difference
between being an answer and being a tool.

00:33:01.970 --> 00:33:02.970
And history is a tool.

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:05.096
SIERRA: We use history to center ourselves
on a timeline, to evaluate where we’ve been

00:33:05.096 --> 00:33:07.649
and where we’re headed. There’s a quote
about all of this that keeps popping up in

00:33:07.649 --> 00:33:13.721
my head. It’s from a short story by William
Faulkner. “The past is never dead. It's

00:33:13.721 --> 00:33:15.649
not even past.”

00:33:15.649 --> 00:33:20.799
It can seem confusing at first, but to me
it captures how if you understand the past

00:33:20.799 --> 00:33:26.300
- both your own past and human history - you
can see how it continues to inform everything

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:28.220
that happens around you.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:31.610
Knowing history is an empowering thing. It’s
a tool for societies - and societies that

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:36.889
understand their histories well tend to make
better decisions. But it’s also a tool for

00:33:36.889 --> 00:33:42.639
us, as individual human beings, in our own
lives. Having your own sense of history gives

00:33:42.639 --> 00:33:47.260
you agency - it makes it harder for other
people to shape your opinions in malicious

00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:51.429
ways, or for someone to tell you that things
have always been the way they are, when they

00:33:51.429 --> 00:33:52.960
haven’t.

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:57.100
Decades from now, children will be reading
about these years, and the decisions we make,

00:33:57.100 --> 00:34:11.919
in their own history books. Let’s not make
those decisions in the dark.

